From 010bae8915a11cd8bc60cca847d1473529ae2669 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 06:47:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hir_typeck: of several inherent impls matching a type, the one
 whose `where` bounds hold is the candidate

cryptography-rust 48.0.0 (py314-cryptography) src/backend/aead.rs:846, `#[pyo3::pymethods] impl AesCcm
{ #[new] fn new(..) -> CryptographyResult<AesCcm> }`:
    BUG: src/hir_typeck/expr_cs__enum.cpp:1997: TODO: visit - Revisit _PathValue when UfcsInherent has
    multiple options - <pyo3::impl_::pyclass::probes::IsPyClass<Result<AesCcm, CryptographyError>>>::VALUE

pyo3 0.28.3 src/impl_/pyclass/probes.rs has two inherent impls of the probe:
    impl<T> IsPyClass<T> where T: PyClass { pub const VALUE: bool = true; }
    impl<T, E> IsPyClass<Result<T, E>> where T: PyClass { pub const VALUE: bool = true; }
Both match `IsPyClass<Result<AesCcm, CryptographyError>>` by type pattern; rustc's candidates are the
impls whose where clauses hold, which leaves the second (two holding would be an ambiguity error).
0324 taught Resolve UFCS that rule for the inherent-vs-trait decision; typeck still counted the impls
that match by type and gave up at two.

Fix: the 0324 helper moves to src/hir_typeck/impl_bounds.{hpp,cpp} (HIR_InherentImplBoundsHold,
a generic parameter counts as undecidable when the resolver carries no generics), with a selector
HIR_SelectInherentImpl (of several impls with the item: typeck's recorded impl parameter count when it
tells them apart, else the one whose bounds hold; null = keep the first match as before). Typeck's
value-path revisit and the call cache select by bounds (with a static resolver carrying the item's
generics); the validation pass, StaticTraitResolve::get_value (consteval, MIR, trans) and Resolve UFCS's
pattern constants select the same impl instead of the first with the item, which asserted
"Path impl_params cache is missized - 2 != 1" once typeck had chosen the second.

Assisted-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
